---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SITE } from "../data/site";
import { EDITORIAL_AUTHOR_SCHEMA, PUBLISHER_SCHEMA } from "../data/editorialGovernance";

const title = "How Echo Buddha Creates Content";
const description =
  "Learn how Echo Buddha creates original Buddhist-inspired articles, quotes, reflections, and meditation guidance with source-aware review.";
const canonicalPath = "/how-echo-buddha-creates-content/";
const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebPage",
  name: title,
  description,
  url: `${SITE.url}${canonicalPath}`,
  author: EDITORIAL_AUTHOR_SCHEMA,
  publisher: PUBLISHER_SCHEMA
};
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} canonicalPath={canonicalPath} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container prose">
      <a class="back-link" href="/editorial-policy/">Editorial Policy</a>
      <span class="eyebrow">Trust and governance</span>
      <h1>How Echo Buddha Creates Content</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Echo Buddha publishes original educational writing and reflective practice support for ordinary life, with
        extra care around Buddhist sources, quote origins, and meditation safety.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container prose">
      <h2>What Echo Buddha Is</h2>
      <p>
        Echo Buddha is an educational and reflective website. It offers plain-English Buddhist-inspired articles,
        quote meanings, meditation guidance, dictionary entries, Dhammapada reflections, and daily practice prompts
        for general readers.
      </p>
      <p>
        The site is meant to be a calm starting point, not a substitute for Buddhist teachers, temples, practice
        communities, qualified medical or mental health care, legal advice, or personal spiritual direction.
      </p>

      <h2>Original Writing and Reflection</h2>
      <p>
        Echo Buddha content is written in original language for this site. Articles and quote stories may draw on
        Buddhist themes such as mindfulness, compassion, patience, impermanence, non-harming, and the path of practice,
        but they should not copy other publishers or present paraphrase as translation.
      </p>
      <p>
        Current quote pages are original Echo Buddha reflections unless a page clearly says otherwise. The
        <a href="/quote-attribution-policy/">Quote Attribution Policy</a> explains how that boundary is protected.
      </p>

      <h2>Review Before Publishing</h2>
      <p>
        Pages are reviewed for clarity, usefulness, tone, duplicate intent, unsupported claims, internal links,
        source notes, and whether the page fits Echo Buddha's role as a Buddhist wisdom and daily reflection companion.
      </p>

      <h2>Publication Workflow</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Define the reader need and confirm which page owns that search intent.</li>
        <li>Identify source-sensitive claims and collect the most direct suitable source context.</li>
        <li>Prepare original explanation, examples, or reflection in the site's own language.</li>
        <li>Check source-to-claim fit, attribution, translation boundaries, terminology, and tradition-specific limits.</li>
        <li>Apply safety and scope checks for meditation, wellbeing, relationship, legal, or health-adjacent material.</li>
        <li>Check usefulness, duplication, internal links, template fit, structured data, and technical behavior.</li>
        <li>Publish under the organizational byline and correct material issues when they are found.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Doctrinal pages receive source-aware review. Quote pages receive origin review. Meditation and wellbeing pages
        receive safety review. These reviews are editorial checks, not claims of academic, monastic, clinical, legal,
        or institutional certification.
      </p>

      <h2>Source-Aware Buddhist Writing</h2>
      <p>
        When Echo Buddha explains Buddhist teachings, the goal is to use respectful beginner language while pointing
        readers toward reputable source context where appropriate. Traditional texts, translations, commentaries,
        paraphrases, and Echo Buddha explanations should remain visibly distinct.
      </p>
      <p>
        Read <a href="/buddhist-sources-and-citations/">Buddhist Sources and Citations</a> for the source standards
        used across Learn pages, articles, dictionary entries, and Dhammapada reflections.
      </p>

      <h2>AI and Automation Boundaries</h2>
      <p>
        AI-assisted and automated tools may help organize research, prepare outlines, support drafting, analyze
        editorial consistency, detect duplicated language, check source and link records, and run technical
        validation. This assistance does not become a credential or source. Echo Buddha remains accountable for the
        final selection, claims, wording, publishing decision, and correction handling.
      </p>
      <p>
        Echo Buddha should not use automation to fabricate Buddhist sources, invented reviewers, credentials,
        translations, quote origins, medical claims, traffic claims, or AdSense approval claims.
      </p>

      <h2>Who Is Responsible</h2>
      <p>
        “Echo Buddha Editorial” is the organizational publication byline, not a named person or a claim that a hidden
        team or expert panel exists. Echo Buddha is responsible for published material through the contact and
        corrections channels on this site. Internal review is not outside certification unless a page explicitly
        names a verified reviewer and defines the review scope.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections and Updates</h2>
      <p>
        Readers can report unclear wording, typos, broken links, source concerns, quote attribution concerns, or safety
        concerns through the <a href="/corrections/">Corrections and Editorial Updates</a> process.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .back-link {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
</style>
